nts for each task to ensure they fit within the four-point budget, verifying that srd110-pr's requirements and srd111-ptx's requirements each stay at or under the limit.\n\nNow I need to generate the YAML output, making sure to order the tasks canonically—documentation before code, and within code tasks, alphabetically by utility path and then by requirement ID, so the pr tasks come before ptx tasks. I'm reconsidering whether additional source files beyond main.go might be needed for more complex utilities, but since I don't have the full SRD details, I'll stick with main.go and trust the stitch agent to create extra files if the requirements demand it. Now I'm ready to write out the final YAML with detailed descriptions that give the agent enough context to work independently.\n\nI'm wrestling with whether to explicitly note that test files shouldn't be modified—it's an execution constraint from the planning constitution rather than a functional requirement, and the stitch agent should already know this from the execution constitution it receives, so I'll leave it out rather than invent a design decision.\n\nSince the files list is exhaustive and only includes main.go, the agent won't touch test files anyway. Now I need to verify the SRD file naming convention matches the docs path structure—srd110-pr.yaml and srd111-ptx.yaml based on the roadmap names.\n\nFor the required_reading section, I'll reference the SRDs since they contain the requirement details, though I'm uncertain about the exact path for requirements.yaml. The design decisions worth including are the SIGPIPE handler installation and existing code patterns, even though they're documented in CLAUDE.md, since they provide helpful clarity for this specific task. I'm working through the weight distribution across these requirement groups. Since the 15.0 restructuring moved each weight-4 requirement into its own group, I can infer the weights: srd110-pr's R3.1 and srd111-ptx's R3.1 are both standalone weight-4 requirements, while the other groups contain lighter requirements that sum to around 3 points total. Now I need to verify this arithmetic against the task constraints. The weight assumption is critical here—I need to make that explicit in my output since the constraint requires showing my arithmetic. I'm also noticing a tension with P9's acceptance criteria granularity targets: my tasks fall below the recommended 5-8 requirements and acceptance criteria range because the weight constraint and sub-requirement limits force me to keep tasks smaller. I should acknowledge this trade-off in my reasoning.\n\nFor acceptance criteria, I can expand to the target range by including build validation, linting checks, SRD requirement verification, regression testing, the SIGPIPE handler implementation, and compiler warning checks. For design decisions, I'm currently at 2-3 (following existing patterns, SIGPIPE handling, and standard I/O patterns), which is below the 3-5 target, but without the actual SRD content, I'm limited in what architectural decisions I can define.\n\nI should also verify that all proposed R-items have \"ready\" status before finalizing, and I'll need to state my weight assumptions since I can't access the requirements.yaml file. Let me now follow the task steps in order: analyze context, summarize state, and reason through the implementation approach.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":43281,"cache_read_input_tokens":11845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":43281},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"31b28c23-d664-4ca8-8c4e-e1301b4a4b6d","uuid":"beb63c2e-cf52-4770-9f2a-133361169a5e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017QasKGGbW7354gbg2P1u94","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation system.\n\n**Architecture**: `cmd/<name>/` holds one package per utility (main.go + test file). `pkg/testutils/`, `pkg/sys/`, `pkg/format/` provide shared infrastructure. `docs/specs/software-requirements/` contains per-utility SRDs. Every utility is verified by running side-by-side with the GNU reference binary (e.g., `gpr`, `gptx`) and comparing stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n\n**Implementation state**: ~70% of utilities have code. Many releases (00.0 through 15.0) are partially or fully implemented. The `requirement_states` field constrains the current batch to only those R-items with status \"ready.\"\n\n**Current release focus**: `requirement_states` lists only two SRDs with ready requirements:\n- **srd110-pr** (release 15.0): R3.1, R4.1, R4.2, R4.3, R5.1, R5.2\n- **srd111-ptx** (release 15.0): R3.1, R4.1, R4.2, R5.1, R5.2\n\nBoth are in release 15.0 (\"complex utilities\"). R1 and R2 requirements are absent from `requirement_states`, indicating they were completed by prior stitch tasks in this generation run. All other utilities either have no ready requirements or are fully complete.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nOnly srd110-pr and srd111-ptx have \"ready\" R-items. Both are in release 15.0, which is in scope. No earlier releases have actionable requirements remaining. The two utilities are independent (no cross-dependency), so pr and ptx tasks can run in parallel.\n\nPer P2, implementation and tests are split into separate tasks. This batch proposes implementation tasks only; test tasks will follow in a future batch.\n\n## Weight Budget Checks\n\nRelease 15.0 description states: \"All SRDs have been restructured so each weight-4 requirement is in its own group.\" R3.1 stands alone in its group for both SRDs, so I assume weight 4. Requirements in multi-member groups (R4.x, R5.x) assume weight 1 each.\n\n```\nTask 0: srd110-pr R3.1 (w=4)\nTotal weight: 4 → ≤ 4 ✓\n\nTask 1: srd110-pr R4.1 (w=1) + R4.2 (w=1) + R4.3 (w=1) + R5.1 (w=1)\nTotal weight: 1+1+1+1 = 4 → ≤ 4 ✓\n\nTask 2: srd111-ptx R3.1 (w=4)\nTotal weight: 4 → ≤ 4 ✓\n\nTask 3: srd111-ptx R4.1 (w=1) + R4.2 (w=1) + R5.1 (w=1) + R5.2 (w=1)\nTotal weight: 1+1+1+1 = 4 → ≤ 4 ✓\n```\n\n**Deferred to next batch**: srd110-pr R5.2 (would exceed 4-task limit).\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/pr: implement srd110 R3.1\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd110-pr.yaml (R3.1 requirements and acceptance criteria)\n      - cmd/pr/main.go (existing implementation from prior requirements R1-R2)\n      - pkg/sys/ (InstallSIGPIPEHandler and related utilities)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/pr/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add R3.1 feature to existing pr implementation\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd110-pr requirement R3.1 exactly as specified in the SRD, including all sub-criteria and edge cases defined for this requirement\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the existing code structure and patterns established by prior requirements (R1, R2) already implemented in cmd/pr/main.go\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Ensure sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() is called at the start of main per the shared SIGPIPE protocol\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Write errors to stderr using fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...) and set exit codes per the SRD exit-code contract\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go build -o bin/pr ./cmd/pr/ succeeds with no er